WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.940
A study of over 95,000 people links more REM sleep to lower risk of 83 diseases. This is Planetterrian Daily, episode 187. A study analyzed real-world sleep patterns for more than 95,000 UK biobank participants. 6-8 hours of sleep duration is associated with reduced risk of multiple diseases. More REM sleep was linked to lower risk of 8-3 diseases, including dementia, Parkinson's, and heart failure.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:35.760
Here, more deep sleep was associated with lower risk of 7 diseases such as type 2 diabetes

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:37.840
and major depressive disorder.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.400
The work appeared in PLOS medicine.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.120
Researchers tracked objective sleep metrics rather than self-reports alone.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.040
The large cohort allowed separation of sleep stage effects from total duration.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.180
Something most people get wrong is treating all sleep as interchangeable downtime.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:00.420
Most people assume eight hours of rest is enough, regardless of how the night is structured.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:06.500
Right now, your brain cycles through REM and deep sleep stages that each regulate distinct systems.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:16.120
The UK Biobank Analysis of over 95,000 participants showed REM sleep tracks with lower incidents across 83 separate conditions,

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.620
while deep sleep tracks with only 7.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.500
That split reveals why total sleep time alone misses the protective biology.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:31.500
A listener can track personal sleep architecture with a consumer wearable and adjust evening routines to protect the later REM window.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.520
Now, shifting to an engineered system built for target discovery.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:41.580
The first release of a virtual biotech system deployed more than 37,000 AI agents.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:48.800
The agents identified targets and forecasted clinical trial success for both efficacy and side-effect avoidance.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.280
Results were published in Science magazine.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:57.220
seen. The system tested predictions across multiple therapeutic areas. James Zoo and Harrison

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:02.880
Zong led the effort. One early accomplishment involved ranking candidate molecules by predicted

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:08.680
safety profiles. The approach combined target discovery with trial outcome modeling in a single

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:15.540
workflow. While AI speeds up target finding, a rare inherited variant shows how directly genetics

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:21.500
can drive disease risk even without external factors. Researchers identified a rare germline

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:28.320
EGFR variant that confers a 25-fold risk of lung cancer. The risk exceeds that from smoking

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:34.320
and appears in non-smokers. The variant traces to a founder event in southern Appalachia.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:40.300
The finding is featured on the cover of science. Carriers showed elevated incidents even without

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:45.480
tobacco exposure. The mutation affects the epidermal growth factor receptor pathway

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:51.800
directly. Genetic tracing placed the origin several generations ago in a specific regional population.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.240
Genetic risk is fixed but daily behaviors like post-exercise heat exposure can still shift

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:03.320
physiological outcomes. A company tested interventions to improve workforce sleep.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.680
Passive heat exposure is recommended as close as possible to the end of a training session

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:15.100
while body temperature remains elevated. The report appeared in the economist. Workers who aligned

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.400
heat sessions with post-exercise recovery reported better rest metrics.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.720
The protocol leverages residual core temperature elevation from training.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.740
Company data tracked sleep quality before and after the change.

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:32.440
The approach integrates with existing exercise routines without added equipment.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:38.800
A new meta-analysis suggests passive heat after resistance training can increase muscle hypertrophy.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.360
Heat acts as a cellular stressor that raises heat shock protein expression

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.520
and activates M-tore signaling.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.540
The analysis reviewed multiple human trials.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.840
Brad Schoenfeld contributed to the meta-analysis.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:55.060
Participants who got it post-lift heat sessions

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.680
showed greater muscle cross-sexual area gains.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:01.340
The mechanism involves both protein synthesis pathways

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:03.440
and cellular stress responses.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:07.900
Typical protocols use 20 minutes at 180 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.860
Heat as a cellular stressor leads naturally

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:14.600
to another cellular defense mechanism recently uncovered in the brain.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:21.520
Cambridge and MRC laboratory researchers identified a cellular defense that marks abnormal glycogen

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:22.320
for destruction.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:27.100
The pathway prevents accumulation linked to severe neurological disorders.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:31.880
The ubiquitin tag directs faulty glycogen to degradation machinery.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:38.000
University of Cambridge and MRC laboratory of molecular biology collaborated on the study.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.300
The mechanism operates continuously in neurons.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:46.640
Fizz.org detailed the discovery of this previously unknown protective system.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.640
The finding opens new angles on glycogen storage disorders.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:59.180
From individual biology to population level patterns, a new survey shows how Americans already connect environmental change to health.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:04.580
The trade explains why people with similar brain pathologies show different symptom severity.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:08.500
Nature published the Finding Online on September 18.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:12.940
Researchers measured cognitive reserve markers in patient cohorts.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.860
The work identifies a modifiable factor in disease expression.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:20.800
Brain imaging and cognitive testing data supported the link.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:24.160
The publication appears in the journal's online edition.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:27.900
The finding separates pathology burden from clinical outcome.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:33.360
A survey from the Annenberg Public Policy Center found nearly two-thirds of Americans

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.300
believed climate change increases respiratory and heat-related illnesses.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.920
The poll was conducted after the hottest summer on record.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:44.640
Respondents also cited insect-borne disease risks.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.120
The University of Pennsylvania Center released the national sample results.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:53.160
Two and three adults connected extreme heat directly to health burdens.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:58.060
The timing of the survey captured public perception during peak summer temperatures.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:01.680
Findings appear in Fizz.org coverage of the report.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:08.120
Fort. Scientists described the Tilcaio Tiger Cat as a distinct species. The discovery marks the

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:13.640
first new Wild Cat species identified in more than a century. Bolivian researchers led the

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:19.740
field work. The animal was distinguished by coat pattern and genetic markers. The find updates

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:26.120
mammalian taxonomy in South America. Camera trap images and tissue samples confirmed separation

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:32.100
from known populations. Publication details the morphological and molecular evidence used for

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:39.280
classification. Every additional 100 kilograms of vehicle weight increased fatality odds by 6%.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:45.080
Every additional 10 centimeters of vehicle height increased fatality odds by 11%.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:51.390
The study examined crash data across multiple regions. Vehicle size metrics were matched to

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:57.910
and outcomes. The analysis controlled for speed and impact location. Results were posted on the

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:03.450
science subreddit with links to the underlying paper. The trend held across urban and suburban

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:09.570
collision records. The European Parliament, Commission and Council signed a joint declaration in June

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:15.910
making culture a strategic priority. Cultural activities are now positioned as tools for public health.

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:20.250
The declaration was signed on the sidelines of the European Council in Brussels.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:25.010
Museums and galleries are being evaluated for prescription-style referrals.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:29.310
The move integrates arts participation into broader wellness frameworks.

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:34.190
Fizz.org reported on the policy shift and its implementation timeline.

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:37.510
Member states are now developing pilot programs.

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:43.150
K-A-I-S-T researchers discovered graphene oxide liquid crystals in 2011.

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:48.530
15 years of subsequent work produced fibers with high strength and thermal conductivity

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:51.070
now entering thermal management applications.

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:55.710
Early products included antibacterial toothbrushes and sportswear.

00:07:56.410 --> 00:08:01.330
The material properties allow efficient heat dissipation in electronics and aircraft components.

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:05.990
The original liquid crystal discovery enabled scalable spinning processes.

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:10.270
Current research focuses on aerospace thermal management systems.

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:15.090
Fizz.org traced the progression from laboratory finding to commercial use.

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:20.650
Glacier Lost Day arrived on June 29, three months earlier than in a healthy state.

00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:26.250
The date marks when winter snow accumulation is fully melted and net mass loss begins.

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:31.430
Matthias Hus of Glamos described the timing as three months too early.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:35.530
Swiss glaciers reached the threshold after accelerated spring melt.

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:39.310
The network monitors mass balance across multiple sites.

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:43.450
Fizz.org reported the record based on GLAMOS measurements.

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:47.490
The early date signals cumulative mass deficit for the season.

00:08:48.350 --> 00:08:54.250
New EU packaging rules taking effect in 2030 ban single-use plastic in food service.

00:08:54.830 --> 00:09:00.030
Pilot projects show hotels can reduce both waste volumes and operating expenses by switching

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:01.270
to reusable systems.

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:05.390
The regulation was published August 12th.

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:09.150
Properties tracked reductions in packaging purchases and disposal fees.

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:13.050
The shift aligns with the packaging and packaging waste regulation.

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:18.070
Fizz.org covered results from early adopters in several European countries.

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:22.450
Cost savings came primarily from lower single use item procurement.

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:28.350
Suncune Quon University chemists developed two new chiral-organic catalyst systems.

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:33.570
The methods allow precise construction of complex molecules with high stereo control.

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:36.450
Do-Hune Reuse team reported the work?

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:40.810
The catalysts operate under mild conditions and avoid metal residues.

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:46.050
Target molecules include pharmaceutical intermediates requiring specific spatial arrangements.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:50.070
Fizz.org summarized the advance from the Department of Chemistry.

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:53.730
Both methods expand the toolkit for asymmetric synthesis.

